{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>Of a talent of pure gold shall he make it, with all these vessels.</b> The menorah together with its lamps weighed a talent, but the rest of the vessels, such as the tongs and the snuffdishes, were not from the talent but of pure gold. And the reading of the verse is: ככר זהב טהור יעשה אותה — 'a talent of pure gold shall he make it,' that is, the body of the menorah and its framework, which are the branches coming out from its sides; זהב טהור יעשה את כל הכלים האלה — 'of pure gold shall he make all these vessels.' And this comes to explain that the vessels are not included in the weight of the talent." "<b>And likewise</b> the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote: ככר זהב טהור יעשה אותה את כל הכלים האלה — 'he shall make all these vessels' of pure gold. And so he mentioned in the account of its making: ככר זהב טהור עשה אותה ואת כל כליה — that is, ואת כל כליה — 'and all its vessels' — of pure gold, not from the talent, for the weight of the talent applies only to the menorah and its lamps alone. And so we learned in Menachot: 'ככר זהב טהור' — and it further says, יעשה את כל הכלים האלה — the menorah and its lamps come from the talent, but its tongs and its snuffdishes do not come from the talent. And how do I establish את כל הכלים האלה — that they should all be of gold.'" "<b>And know that</b> the Gaon Rabbeinu Chananel, of blessed memory, explained in his commentary on the Torah by way of allusion in these vessels: that the Ark alludes to the righteous sage, who must be תוכו כברו — his inside like his outside — just as the Ark was overlaid with gold inside and outside. And just as the base of the Ark was an amah and a half by an amah and a half, which in the Talmudic measure is four amot less a quarter-amah, so the righteous man must diminish from himself the four elements in the body and increase in his righteousness and good deeds. And just as the walls of the Ark were twelve amot — for the long walls are seven and a half amot and the walls along the width four and a half amot — so the righteous man is worthy of fulfilling the twelve things spelled out in the psalm (Psalms 15:1), 'HaShem, who shall sojourn in Your tent.' Above the righteous is the rank of the prophets, and corresponding to them the kapooret upon the Ark alluded to the prophets, who are above the righteous; and the kapooret was entirely of gold, and so the prophet is entirely righteousness, as it says (Proverbs 8:8): ״בצדק כל אמרי פי״. The keruvim upon the kapooret alluded to the angels, who are above the prophets, and they spread their wings upward, alluding to the Shekhinah, which is above the angels, for the angels are a throne of glory to receive the glory of the Shekhinah; and this is what is written, אל הכפורת יהיו פני הכרובים — that their faces would be looking downward at the kapooret, and they were not permitted to look upward, and this is what is written, אל הכפורת יהיו פני הכרובים. The Holy One, blessed be He, is above all, as it says (II Samuel 6:2): ״אשר נקרא שם שם ה' צבאות יושב הכרובים עליו״. And so Solomon did in the Temple — keruvim of wood, as it says (I Kings 6:23): ״ויעש בדביר שני כרובים עצי שמן״. And the Table corresponds to the kings of Israel, at whose table many of Israel recline, as it says (I Kings 2:7): ״ולבני ברזילי הגלעדי תעשה חסד והיו באוכלי שלחנך״, and it is written (I Kings 5:2 [5:25 in some editions]): ״ויהי לחם שלמה ליום אחד״ and so forth. And it was overlaid with pure gold, like the nature of kingship, which exists only outwardly, such as Saul, who said to Samuel (I Samuel 15:30): ״כבדני נא נגד זקני עמי ונגד ישראל״. And its length was two amot, corresponding to the two things required of the king — justice and war — as it says (I Samuel 8:20): ״ושפטנו מלכנו ויצא לפנינו ונלחם את מלחמתנו״. The measure of its walls was nine amot, corresponding to the nine commandments with which the king was charged (Deuteronomy 17:16): ״לא ירבה לו סוסים״, ״ולא ישיב את העם מצרימה״, ״ולא ירבה לו נשים״, ״ולא יסור לבבו״, ״וכסף וזהב לא ירבה לו״, ״וכתב לו את משנה התורה הזאת״, ״וקרא בו כל ימי חייו״, ״לבלתי רום לבבו מאחיו״, ״ולבלתי סור מן המצוה ימין ושמאל״. And the rim was so that the showbread would not slip from it, and it served to guard what was on the table, corresponding to the priests and prophets who guard the kings lest they stumble, as it says (II Chronicles 24:2): ״ויעש יואש הישר בעיני ה' כל ימי יהוידע הכהן״, and it is written of Uzziah (II Chronicles 26:5): ״ויהי לדרוש אלהים בימי זכריהו המבין בראות האלהים״. And all the matters of these vessels — the Ark and the Table and the Menorah and the Altar of Burnt Offering and the Golden Altar: the Ark corresponds to the righteous, those who grasp the Torah; the Table corresponds to the kings of Israel; the Menorah corresponds to the sages of Israel; the Golden Altar corresponds to the priests; the Altar of Burnt Offering corresponds to Israel. Thus far."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:47.273754945Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}